WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
Most carbon atoms are carbon-12.

2
00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:06.020
In NMR, they give no signal at all.

3
00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:12.240
Only about 1.1 per cent are carbon-13,
and only those nuclei give a signal.

4
00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:17.560
Yet a carbon-13 spectrum shows a peak
for every kind of carbon in a compound.

5
00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:22.180
So how can about one carbon atom in
a hundred be enough to map them all?

6
00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:25.360
In this mission, you'll
count carbon environments,

7
00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:29.040
using symmetry to find
carbons that share one peak.

8
00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.040
Then you'll read where each peak sits,

9
00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:35.960
and use carbon and hydrogen spectra
together to propose a structure.

10
00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:42.270
Your mission: explain how so few carbon-13
atoms can map every carbon in a compound.